// @no-instantiate invalid local index
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. A literal-union atom in a SHADOWING inner binding, widened to a
// string, writes to a scratch local that was never declared. V8 (the harness engine) reports
// `invalid local index: 6`; wasmtime reports `unknown local 6`.
// `vl check --codegen` exits 0.
//
// THE SCAN AND THE EMIT RESOLVE THE NAME DIFFERENTLY. Six sinks widen an atom to its member
// string, and each is a PAIR: an emit arm that calls `emitAtomToStr`, and a reservation
// predicate that tells `emit_classify`'s scan to allocate the string-op scratch frame the widen
// stages into. Both sides ask `exprIsLitAtom`, whose `Ident` arm resolves through
// `declaredSlotOf` — and that falls back to a LINEAR `localNames` scan returning the FIRST
// match.
//
// The scan runs before the body is emitted, so the scope stack is empty and the OUTER `v` (an
// i32) wins: the predicate says "not an atom" and no frame is reserved. By emit time
// `scopeBind` has bound the inner `v: K`, so the emit arm fires and writes
// `strScratchBase + 3` into a frame that does not exist.
//
// This is pinned at the `call` sink (`show(v)`) deliberately, because that sink is OLD — the
// defect predates the push sink added alongside this file and belongs to all six siblings
// (`callWidensAtomToStr`, `retWidensAtomToStr`, `globalInitWidensAtomToStr`,
// `letWidensAtomToStr`, `assignWidensAtomToStr`, `pushWidensAtomToStr`). Pinning it on the
// newest one would have read as a regression introduced with it, which it is not.
//
// WORSE THAN A LOUD FAILURE, SOMETIMES. The stolen slot is out of bounds here, so the module is
// rejected. When the function happens to declare enough locals — an i32-keyed map frame will do
// it — the index lands IN BOUNDS on an i32 slot and the module VALIDATES, leaving the atom id
// written over an unrelated local. That form is silent.
//
// THE FIX IS ONE LEVEL DOWN and closes all six at once: make `declaredSlotOf` resolve a name
// the same way during the scan as during the emit. It is not attempted here because it is a
// name-resolution change under six consumers, not a sink fix.
type K = "ka" | "kb"
function show(s: string) { print(s) }
function f(n: i32) {
  const v = 7
  if n > 0 {
    const v: K = "kb"
    show(v)
  }
  return v
}
function main() { print(f(1)) }
main()
